  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Catching up with Elena in the hotel lobby, Chef Barinov finally admits that the woman in the dining room was a stranger and that Eleonora is his real ex-wife. Elena calmly informs him she already learned that at the gallery.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лена, прости.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это была не она, это не моя жена.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Бывшая моя жена — это Элеонора.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "А, наконец-то.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Лучше поздно, чем никогда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Витя, она мне рассказала всё ещё на выставке.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Обещаю, я больше никогда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Не надо ничего обещать мне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я просто запаниковал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "А потом почему не признался?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Боялся вот этого разговора.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Слушай, я забыла сумку, я быстро.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лена, прости.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lena, forgive me.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'Lena' before landing on 'forgive'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это была не она, это не моя жена.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That was not her, that is not my wife.' The '-a' ending on the past-tense verb for 'was' marks a female speaker or subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Бывшая моя жена — это Элеонора.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My ex-wife is Eleonora.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'ex-wife', while 'Eleonora' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "А, наконец-то.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Ah, finally.' The grammar packs 'and' and 'at last' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Лучше поздно, чем никогда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Better late than never.' The meaning hinges on 'better' working together with 'never' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Витя, она мне рассказала всё ещё на выставке.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vitya, she told me everything back at the exhibition.' In Russian, the idea of 'proper name' flows naturally into 'exhibition' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Обещаю, я больше никогда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I promise, I never will again.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'promise' and finishing on 'never'. Elena answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Не надо ничего обещать мне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You do not need to promise me anything.' Elena anchors the line on 'not', while 'to me' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я просто запаниковал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I simply panicked.' The grammar packs 'I' and 'panicked' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "А потом почему не признался?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And why did you not confess afterward?' The '-l' ending on the verb meaning 'confessed' places the action in the masculine past tense. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Боялся вот этого разговора.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I was afraid of this very conversation.' The meaning hinges on 'was afraid' working together with 'conversation' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Слушай, я забыла сумку, я быстро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Listen, I forgot my bag, I will be quick.' The meaning hinges on 'listen' working together with 'quickly' in spoken Russian. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
